<h2>1.2 Detailed Design &amp; UML Models</h2>

<h3>1.2.1 Component Interaction Architecture</h3>
<p>
  The view layer avoids global state bloat by maintaining unidirectional data flow. Top-level state flows down as immutable props, while child components trigger mutations via strongly-typed callbacks.
</p>
<div class="uml-box">
  +---------------------------------------------------------------------+
  |                               App.tsx                               |
  |   - Holds current user state, selectedMonth, selectedView           |
  |   - Consumes useFinanceData(user, selectedMonth)                    |
  +---------------------------------------------------------------------+
        |                     |                   |                 |
        v                     v                   v                 v
  +------------+       +-------------+     +-------------+    +---------------+
  | Navbar.tsx |       |DashboardView|     | BudgetView  |    |ReportsView.tsx|
  | - MonthNav |       | - KPI Cards |     | - Progress  |    | - 12-Mo Bar   |
  | - ViewTabs |       | - Donut SVG |     | - Variance  |    | - Yearly CSV  |
  | - Drawer   |       | - Trajectory|     | - Overspend |    | - Monthly CSV |
  +------------+       +-------------+     +-------------+    +---------------+
</div>

<h3>1.2.2 State Machine Diagram: Category Budget Lifecycle</h3>
<p>
  Each expense category dynamically transitions across deterministic operational states based on the budget consumption equation: <em>Utilization % = (Spent / Limit) * 100</em>.
</p>
<div class="uml-box">
             +------------------+
             |    [Initial]     |
             | No Budget Limit  |
             +------------------+
                      |
                      | Set Monthly Limit (limit > 0)
                      v
             +------------------+
             |   UNDER_BUDGET   |<-----------------------+
             |  (Spend < 90%)   |                        |
             |   Color: Emerald |                        |
             +------------------+                        |
                      |                                  |
                      | Log Expense (90% <= Spend < 100%)| Expense Deleted
                      v                                  | or Limit Increased
             +------------------+                        |
             |   NEAR_LIMIT     |------------------------+
             | (90% - 99.9% Usg)|                        |
             |   Color: Amber   |                        |
             +------------------+                        |
                      |                                  |
                      | Log Expense (Spend >= 100%)      |
                      v                                  |
             +------------------+                        |
             |   OVER_BUDGET    |------------------------+
             | (Spend >= Limit) |
             |   Color: Crimson |
             +------------------+
</div>

<h2>1.3 Design Decisions &amp; Trade-Off Studies</h2>

<h3>Trade-Off Study 1: Persistence Layer — Cloud Firestore vs. Relational Database (PostgreSQL)</h3>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Criteria</th>
    <th>Cloud Firestore (Selected)</th>
    <th>PostgreSQL / Cloud SQL</th>
    <th>Decision Rationale</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Data Model</strong></td>
    <td>Hierarchical Document Model (`/users/{uid}/...`)</td>
    <td>Normalized Relational Tables with Foreign Keys</td>
    <td>Personal finance records are naturally tenant-isolated. Path-based subcollections guarantee zero cross-user leakage.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Real-Time Sync</strong></td>
    <td>Native WebSocket Snapshot Listeners (`onSnapshot`)</td>
    <td>Requires custom polling or server-side WebSockets</td>
    <td>Firestore provides instant sub-second synchronization across multiple devices without custom socket servers.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Cold Start &amp; Latency</strong></td>
    <td>Near-zero cold start; distributed edge delivery</td>
    <td>Connection pooling and server provisioning latency</td>
    <td>Instant load times are essential for mobile quick-logging of expenses.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Infrastructure Maintenance</strong></td>
    <td>Serverless, zero-ops, automatic scaling</td>
    <td>Requires connection sizing, migrations, DB maintenance</td>
    <td>Firestore eliminates operational overhead while maintaining enterprise availability.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3>Trade-Off Study 2: Application Architecture — Client-Side SPA (Vite + React) vs. Server-Side Rendering (SSR / Next.js)</h3>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Criteria</th>
    <th>Client-Side SPA with Vite (Selected)</th>
    <th>Full SSR Framework</th>
    <th>Decision Rationale</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Interactive Latency</strong></td>
    <td>Sub-millisecond chart re-renders and client filtering</td>
    <td>Server round-trips for parameterized view toggling</td>
    <td>Financial dashboards require immediate responsiveness when adjusting date sliders and chart filters.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Security Boundary</strong></td>
    <td>Direct SDK interaction authenticated via client JWTs</td>
    <td>Server-side API session proxies with secret keys</td>
    <td>Using Firebase Auth with rules-enforced Firestore access eliminates middleman API server vulnerabilities.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Bundle &amp; Build Velocity</strong></td>
    <td>Lightweight ES Module bundling (&lt; 3 seconds)</td>
    <td>Complex Node runtime compilation &amp; SSR polyfills</td>
    <td>Vite provides fast developer feedback and clean production asset distribution.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3>Trade-Off Study 3: Report Generation — Client-Side Streaming vs. Server-Side File Generation</h3>
<p>
  <strong>Decision:</strong> Aura Money implements a client-side RFC 4180 CSV engine directly in the browser using HTML5 Blob URLs.
  <br><strong>Rationale:</strong> Generating CSV files on the client avoids transmitting sensitive transactional data to an external PDF/CSV microservice. It allows zero-latency downloads, functions offline from cached snapshots, and eliminates backend compute costs.
</p>

